<!-- ===================== SECTION 8: GUPTA EMPIRE ===================== -->
<h2 id="gupta">8. Gupta Empire (320-550 CE)</h2>

<p>The <span class="highlight">Gupta Empire</span> is considered the <strong>'Golden Age'</strong> of ancient India — a period of classical refinement in art, literature, science, and philosophy. The Gupta period (particularly under Chandragupta I, Samudragupta, and Chandragupta II) saw the emergence of the classical norms of Indian civilisation — the iconography of Hindu gods, the code of laws, the style of Sanskrit literature, the form of temple architecture, and the mathematical and astronomical knowledge that would later spread to the Arab world and Europe. The Gupta Empire ruled over most of northern India and the Deccan, though not as vast as the Mauryan Empire — its economic and cultural influence extended far beyond its political boundaries through maritime trade with Southeast Asia and the Byzantine Empire.</p>

<h3>Gupta Chronology and Major Rulers</h3>

<div class="table-wrap"><table>
<tr><th>Ruler</th><th>Reign</th><th>Key Achievements</th></tr>
<tr><td>Srigupta</td><td>c. 240-280 CE</td><td>Founder of the dynasty; ruler of a small principality in Magadha (Pataliputra area); title 'Maharaja'</td></tr>
<tr><td>Ghatotkacha</td><td>c. 280-319 CE</td><td>Son of Srigupta; expanded the kingdom; title 'Maharaja'</td></tr>
<tr><td><strong>Chandragupta I</strong></td><td>319-335 CE</td><td>The first great Gupta ruler; married Kumaradevi, a Lichchhavi princess of Vaishali — the alliance gave him control over Pataliputra and eastern UP. Assumed the imperial title 'Maharajadhiraja'. The Gupta Era (319 CE) is dated from his accession.</td></tr>
<tr><td><strong>Samudragupta</strong></td><td>335-375 CE</td><td>The 'Indian Napoleon' (V. A. Smith). Detailed by the Prayaga Prashasti (Allahabad Pillar Inscription) composed by Harishena, his court poet and minister. Conducted two major campaigns: dharmavijaya (conquest for righteousness — southern campaign) and digvijaya (northern campaign). The Allahabad Pillar lists defeated kings in three categories: those defeated in Aryavarta (northern kings — 9 kings), those captured and released in Dakshinapatha (southern kings — 12 kings), and the frontier kingdoms (atavika rajas - forest kings of central India). His coins depict him playing the veena (indicating his love of music) and performing the Ashvamedha sacrifice (the first Gupta ruler to do so). He patronised poets — his court included Harishena, and probably Kalidasa (though Kalidasa is more securely associated with Chandragupta II). He never lost a battle.</td></tr>
<tr><td><strong>Chandragupta II (Vikramaditya)</strong></td><td>375-415 CE</td><td>The greatest Gupta ruler; reached the empire's territorial peak. Conquered the Western Kshatrapas (Shakas of Ujjain) in c. 409 CE, incorporating Gujarat, Saurashtra, and Malwa — giving the Guptas access to the Arabian Sea ports (Bharuch, Cambay) and the prosperous trade with the West. Assumed the title 'Vikramaditya' (Sun of Valour). His court was the legendary <strong>Navaratna</strong> (Nine Gems): Kalidasa (poet/playwright), Amarasimha (lexicographer — Amarakosha), Dhanvantari (physician — father of Ayurvedic surgery), Varahamihira (astronomer), Vararuchi (grammarian), Shanku (architect), Kshapanaka (astrologer), Vetalabhatta (magician), Ghatakarapara (sculptor). Chinese pilgrim <strong>Faxian (Fa-Hien)</strong> visited India during his reign (399-414 CE), recording a peaceful, prosperous kingdom with no land tax for monks, no capital punishment, and a flourishing Buddhist monastic culture. His gold coinage (dinara) shows him as a hunter, equestrian archer, and king-lion-slayer.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Kumaragupta I</td><td>415-455 CE</td><td>Founded Nalanda University — the most important centre of Buddhist learning in the ancient world. The temple of Manjushri at Nalanda and several monasteries. Coins show him performing the Ashvamedha and as a lion-slayer. His reign was mostly peaceful.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Skandagupta</td><td>455-467 CE</td><td>Defended the empire against the <strong>Huna (Hephthalite White Hun)</strong> invasion. The Junagadh Rock Inscription of Skandagupta records his repair of the Sudarshana lake (which had burst its embankments) and the Hunas 'falling on the earth like a terrible torrent of poisonous water.' He repelled the invasion but the empire was financially exhausted. His Bhitari Pillar Inscription shows his piety and victory. After his death, the empire declined rapidly due to Hun invasions, internal rebellions, and the rise of local feudatories (the Pushyabhutis of Thanesar, the Maukharis of Kannauj, the Vardhanas).</td></tr>
<tr><td>Later Guptas</td><td>467-550 CE</td><td>Budhagupta, Narasimhagupta, Kumaragupta II, Vishnugupta. The Hunas under Toramana and Mihirakula ravaged the western parts of the empire (Gwalior, Malwa, Saurashtra). Yashodharman of Malwa (c. 530 CE) defeated Mihirakula (the Mandasor Pillar Inscription of Yashodharman boasts of his victory). By 550 CE, the Gupta Empire had collapsed into independent kingdoms.</td></tr>
</table></div>

<h3>Gupta Administration</h3>

<p>The Gupta administrative system was decentralised compared to the Mauryas — power was shared with feudatories and local chiefs. The king was <strong>Maharajadhiraja</strong> (Great King of Kings) and <strong>Paramabhattaraka</strong> (Supreme Lord). Provinces were called <strong>Bhuktis</strong> (governed by Uparikas/Provincial Governors), which were divided into <strong>Vishayas</strong> (districts, governed by Vishayapatis/District Officers), then <strong>Nagarashreshthins</strong> (city administrators) and <strong>Gramikas</strong> (village headmen). District-level administration included the Vishayapati, adhikarana (council), a representative of the merchant guild (nagarashreshthin), a representative of the artisans (sarthavaha), the chief scribe (prathama-kayastha), and the head of the district records office (pustapala). The Poona plates of the Vakataka dynasty (contemporary of the Guptas) and the Gupta copper plates (Damodarpur, Baigram, Nalanda, etc.) show this district-level administration in detail — land grants to Brahmins and Buddhist monasteries, tax exemptions for donated lands, and the role of the district council in approving land sales.</p>

<p>The Guptas introduced the concept of <strong>land grants to Brahmins</strong> (brahmadeya/agrahara grants) on a massive scale — these were tax-free grants of uncultivated land to Brahmins for clearing and settling, as recorded in numerous copper-plate inscriptions (the Damodarpur copper plates, the Paharpur plate, the Nalanda plate). This policy stimulated the expansion of agriculture into forested areas but also created a class of Brahmin landlords and weakened central authority over time, as the grant areas became autonomous. The system also established the pattern of early Indian feudalism (debated by historians — R. S. Sharma argued that feudalism began in the Gupta period; D. N. Jha challenged this view). Land revenue was typically 1/6th of the produce, with additional taxes: uparikara (extra tax), bhaga (share), bhoga (periodic tribute), and kara (forced labour).</p>

<h3>Gupta Economy</h3>

<p>The Gupta period witnessed a flourishing of trade, crafts, and agriculture. <strong>Agriculture</strong> benefited from iron ploughshares, well irrigation, and the expansion of cultivation through land grants. Major crops: rice (Ganga valley), wheat and barley (Punjab, UP), cotton (Bengal, Gujarat), sugarcane (UP, Bihar), indigo (Bihar), pepper and spices (Kerala and the Deccan). <strong>Trade</strong> was extensive both inland and overseas. Inland trade was facilitated by the <strong>Uttarapatha</strong> (Northern Route from Taxila to Pataliputra and Tamralipti) and <strong>Dakshinapatha</strong> (Southern Route from Pataliputra to Pratishthana and the Deccan). Temple towns (Mathura, Varanasi, Pataliputra, Ujjain, Vidisha) and port towns (Tamralipti in Bengal, Kalyan/Bharuch on the west coast, Kaveripattinam on the east coast) were centres of commerce. <strong>Overseas trade</strong> with Southeast Asia (Suvarnabhumi — Myanmar/Thailand, and the Malay Archipelago — Kattigara, Java, Sumatra, Bali) was important — Indian merchants established settlements and traded in spices, sandalwood, camphor, and precious stones. Trade with the Byzantine Empire continued through the Red Sea and Persian Gulf. Chinese pilgrim Faxian describes Tamralipti as 'a great emporium on the seacoast.'</p>

<p><strong>Coinage</strong>: The Guptas produced the most beautiful gold coinage in Indian history. The <strong>Dinara</strong> (standard gold coin, 7.8-8.4g) was issued in 8 types by Chandragupta II and Kumaragupta: the Standard Type (king standing, goddess seated on a throne), the Archer Type (king holding a bow — Chandragupta II's most common coin), the Couch Type (king seated on a couch), the Lion-slayer Type (king hunting a lion), the Horseman Type, the Elephant-rider Type, the Ashvamedha Type (Kumaragupta's famous coin showing the sacrificial horse), and the Lyrist Type (Samudragupta playing the veena). Silver coins (Rupaka) were minted for western India (after the conquest of the Western Kshatrapas) and were modelled on Shaka coinage. Copper coins are rare.</p>

<h3>Gupta Culture — The Golden Age</h3>

<p><strong>Literature</strong>: The Gupta period is the classical age of Sanskrit literature. <strong>Kalidasa</strong> (c. 400 CE) — the greatest Sanskrit poet and playwright — wrote <strong>Abhijnanashakuntalam</strong> (The Recognition of Shakuntala — considered the best of India's 1000 Sanskrit plays, famously praised by Goethe: 'Wouldst thou the young and the old, the beautiful and the year combine...'), <strong>Vikramorvasiyam</strong>, <strong>Malavikagnimitram</strong> (plays), <strong>Raghuvamsha</strong>, <strong>Kumarasambhava</strong> (long poems), and <strong>Meghaduta</strong> (The Cloud Messenger — one of the most beautiful lyric poems in world literature). <strong>Shudraka's Mrichchhakatika</strong> (The Little Clay Cart — a social comedy of courtesans, gambling, and rebellion). <strong>Vishakhadatta's Mudrarakshasa</strong> (political drama about Chanakya's diplomacy). <strong>Bharavi's Kiratarjuniya</strong> (epic poem on Arjuna's penance). <strong>Dandin's Dashakumaracharita</strong> (prose romance). <strong>Vatsyayana's Kama Sutra</strong> (c. 3rd-4th century — the most famous treatise on erotica, but also a comprehensive work on the art of living). <strong>Panchatantra</strong> and <strong>Hitopadesha</strong> (animal fables with moral lessons — translated worldwide via Persian (Anwar-i-Suhaili), Arabic (Kalila wa Dimna), and European languages).</p>

<p><strong>Science and Mathematics</strong>: The Gupta period saw extraordinary scientific advances. <strong>Aryabhata</strong> (b. 476 CE at Pataliputra) — author of the <strong>Aryabhatiya</strong> (written at age 23): he calculated pi (3.1416), explained the true cause of solar and lunar eclipses (the earth's shadow on the moon), stated that the earth rotates on its axis, and calculated the length of the solar year as 365.358 days (remarkably accurate). He proposed that the earth is a sphere that rotates daily — a revolutionary idea. <strong>Varahamihira</strong> (c. 505-587 CE at Ujjain) — author of <strong>Brihat Samhita</strong> (encyclopedia of astronomy, astrology, geography, architecture, and natural history) and <strong>Pancha Siddhantika</strong> (the Five Astronomical Systems, reconciling Indian, Greek, Roman, and Egyptian astronomy). <strong>Sushruta</strong> (though earlier — Sushruta Samhita from c. 1st cent. BCE, but revised in Gupta period) — the father of surgery whose text describes 300 surgical procedures, 121 surgical instruments, and rhinoplasty (nose reconstruction — the first recorded plastic surgery in world history). <strong>Charaka Samhita</strong> (c. 3rd century CE) — the foundational text of Ayurvedic medicine, 120 chapters, 8 branches of medicine. <strong>Brahmi numerals</strong> evolved into the Gupta numerals which became the basis of the modern decimal system — the concept of zero as both a placeholder and a number was fully developed by the 5th century (though earlier evidence exists in the Bakhshali manuscript, c. 3rd-4th cent. CE).</p>

<p><strong>Art and Architecture</strong>: The Gupta period defined the classical Indian temple style. <strong>Three temple styles</strong> emerged: the <strong>Nagara</strong> style (curvilinear shikhara — e.g., Dashavatara Temple, Deogarh, c. 500 CE, dedicated to Vishnu as 10 avatars; Bhitargaon brick temple, Kanpur, c. 400 CE — the earliest surviving brick temple with a curvilinear shikhara), the <strong>Dravida</strong> style (pyramidal vimana — e.g., the Mahabalipuram Shore Temple and rathas built by the Pallavas), and the <strong>Vesara</strong> style (hybrid — e.g., the Kailasa Temple at Ellora, later). The <strong>Dashavatara Temple at Deogarh</strong> shows Gupta classicism at its peak — panel reliefs of Gajendra-moksha, Narasimha, Nara-Narayana, and Anantashayin (Vishnu sleeping on the cosmic serpent Shesha). <strong>Ajanta Caves</strong>: Caves 16, 17, 19 (earlier ones: 9, 10, 12 are from the Satavahana period; Gupta ones are 16, 17, 19, 1, 2) — the finest Indian mural paintings surviving from the ancient world, depicting the Jataka tales, with exquisite naturalism, modelling of human figures, and the famous 'Flying Ascetic' and 'The Dying Princess' (the sister of Yashodhara in Ajanta Cave 16). <strong>Mathura School</strong> — the red sandstone sculptures, the <strong>Kapardin Buddha</strong> (seated Buddha from Mathura, c. 5th cent.), the standing Buddhas with the Gupta halo (elaborately carved). <strong>Sarnath School</strong> — the famous <strong>Sarnath Buddha</strong> (c. 5th cent., teaching posture or Dharmachakra-mudra, with a transparent robe attached to the body). The Udayagiri Caves (Madhya Pradesh, c. 400 CE) contain the famous Varaha Avatar panel — Vishnu as a boar rescuing the earth goddess from the cosmic ocean — a masterpiece of Gupta stone carving.</p>

<h3>Decline of the Gupta Empire</h3>

<p>The Gupta Empire declined due to multiple factors: <strong>Huna invasions</strong> (the White Huns/Hephthalites under Toramana and Mihirakula devastated the northwest and central India from 460-530 CE; Mihirakula was particularly feared — described by Chinese pilgrim Sung Yun as 'the destroyer of many Buddhist monasteries and stupas, a persecutor of Buddhism, and a man of terrifying cruelty'). <strong>Internal rebellions</strong> — feudal lords (samantas) became increasingly independent and stopped paying tribute after Skandagupta's death. <strong>Loss of the lucrative western trade</strong> — the conquest of the Western Kshatrapas' ports was lost to the Hunas and later to the Kalachuris and Maitrakas. <strong>Succession disputes</strong> — weak later rulers (Budhagupta, Narasimhagupta) could not control the feudatories. <strong>Rise of regional powers</strong> — the Pushyabhutis (Harsha's predecessors) in Thanesar, the Maukharis in Kannauj, the Later Guptas in Magadha, the Maitrakas of Valabhi (Saurashtra), and the Vardhanas of Thanesar filled the power vacuum. By 550 CE, the Gupta Empire had ceased to exist as a political entity.</p>

<div class="warn-box"><div class="warn-title">Debate: Was the Gupta Period Really a 'Golden Age'?</div><div class="warn-text">The 'Golden Age' label has been criticised by modern historians (e.g., Romila Thapar, R. S. Sharma). <strong>Arguments against:</strong> 1) The Gupta period saw the decline of the gana-sanghas (republics) — democracy was replaced by absolute monarchy. 2) The land-grant system created feudalism and reduced royal authority. 3) Women's status declined (the Dharmashastras of the Gupta period — the Manusmriti's influence peaked — were deeply patriarchal; we see increased sati and child marriage). 4) The caste system became more rigid, with Brahminical dominance reaching its peak. 5) Most scientific advances were in astronomy and mathematics, not in medicine or technology that improved living standards. <strong>Arguments for:</strong> 1) Unprecedented cultural refinement in art, literature, and architecture — establishing classical norms that guided Indian civilisation for centuries. 2) Major scientific discoveries (Aryabhata's heliocentrism, the zero, decimal system, plastic surgery). 3) Extensive trade and prosperity visible in the high quality of gold coinage and the accounts of Faxian. 4) Nalanda became the world's foremost centre of learning.</div></div>

